The {{nihongo|'''Toyota Avalon'''|トヨタ・アバロン|Toyota Abaron|lead=yes}} is a [[full-size car|full-size]] [[Sedan (automobile)|sedan]] manufactured by [[Toyota]], as its largest [[front-wheel drive]] sedan; also its flagship in the United States, Canada, China and the Middle East. The Avalon was also manufactured in Australia from April 2000 until June 2005, when it was replaced in November 2006 by the [[Toyota Aurion|Aurion]]. The first production Avalon was manufactured in September 1994 at the [[Toyota Motor Manufacturing Kentucky|TMMK]] assembly line in [[Georgetown, Kentucky]],<ref name = "KyForward"/> where subsequent generations have been manufactured.

Toyota marketed the front-drive Avalon as a replacement for its rear-drive [[Toyota Cressida|Cressida]], a model discontinued for the American market in 1992. The Cressida was an upper-level, mid-size, [[rear-wheel drive]] sedan. The Avalon has at times overlapped Toyota's models using the same [[Automobile platform|platform]], including the [[Toyota Camry|Camry]] V6 and the [[Lexus ES]]. The third-generation and subsequent generations was distinguished by offering extra legroom due to its extended-length chassis.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2010/07/25/automobiles/autoreviews/25toyota-avalon.html?pagewanted=2 |work=The New York Times |author-first1=John Pearley|author-last1=Huffman|title=Take Me to an Elks Lodge and Don't Step on It |date=2010-07-23}}</ref> From 2013, the Lexus ES was moved to the extended platform to match the Avalon.<ref name=2013es>{{cite web |url=http://rumors.automobilemag.com/new-york-2012-lexus-reveals-2013-es-new-hybrid-variant-120697.html |title=New York 2012: Lexus Reveals 2013 ES, New Hybrid Variant |publisher=automobilemag.com |first=Donny |last=Nordlicht |date=2012-04-04 |access-date=2013-05-27}}</ref>

As of 2013, the Avalon was sold in the United States, Canada, China, South Korea and the Middle East. It was discontinued in the United States in 2022.<ref>{{cite web |title=Toyota Ditches Avalon and Replaces It With Funky, High-Riding 2023 Crown|url=https://www.edmunds.com/car-news/toyota-ditches-avalon-and-replaces-it-with-funky-high-riding-2023-crown.html|website=Edmunds|date=2022-07-14}}</ref>

[[Avalon]] is a legendary island of the [[Arthurian legend]], fitting it in with Toyota's tradition of naming their sedans after variants of the word for "crown" in various languages ([[Toyota Crown|Crown]], [[Toyota Corona|Corona]], [[Toyota Camry|Camry]], [[Toyota Corolla|Corolla]]), types of crowns ([[Toyota Tiara|Tiara]]), or other aspects of royalty ([[Toyota Scepter|Scepter]]).{{citation needed|date=August 2022}}

